- {{TOPIC}}: not on {{EXAM}} ({{SOURCE: Ed #N, a staff post, date | the syllabus, its line}});
  {{WHAT STAYS, if part of it does}}.

- **Scope:** `in`; `in, PDF pp.A-B` (with the last in-scope slide as "PDF p.N = printed M"), or
  `in, PDF pp.A-B, C-D` for two ranges; `in, slides #/A-#/B` for a reveal deck (horizontal slide
  indexes); `concepts only`; `reserved: <exam>`, copied raw and never rendered, extracted or
  opened; `pending: <planned scope>`, not here yet, its scope fixed at intake and written in one
  of the `in` forms when the file arrives; `out`. Notes go in parentheses, and never hold a page
  range (it would go unchecked); `extract_check.py` refuses any other wording.
- **Answers:** `yes` marks a file that holds answers (official solutions, a past exam's key), with
  `solutions to <doc>` in Notes naming the set it answers. Mock writers never read it. Nothing
  published quotes it until the student has attempted that set, which the cell then records as
  `yes, attempted`.
- **Topic cuts** name what the exam leaves out inside an `in` range, which no page range can say
  ("negative weights are not on Exam 1", from a staff post). They are a list under the table, never
  a second table, and scope like the rows: `03-backbone` marks the subsections a cut reaches and
  what of them stays, the notes keep the material it takes out under a `Not on <exam>` banner
  (`04-write`), and the sheets, mocks and cram order leave that material out (a mock written before
  a later cut keeps it, and the cram order's line for that mock says to skip it: `10-polish`).
- **The exam-details file** (a syllabus, an announcement) is `in`: its facts go to CLAUDE.md, and
  `03-backbone` maps its blocks `skip`.
- **Pages** come from `pdfinfo`, which reads a file's metadata, not its pages: a `reserved` row
  gets its count that way and stays unopened.
- **Doc ids** are what citations use (`[D4 p.17]`): keep them short, and never renumber one.
